course: politics
lesson: 3
title: Who can vote, and why children cannot
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Not everyone can vote: in most countries you must be 18, and usually a citizen, and your name must be on the official list of voters; these are rules people chose, and people argue about changing them.
explain: The age line is not a cleverness test: a 9-year-old may know more about planets than Uncle Steve, but the rule is drawn at an age so it is the same for everyone, famous or not, rich or poor.
explain: The rules have changed before: your great-great-gran may not have been allowed to vote just because she was a woman, until campaigners called suffragettes and suffragists won that right.
explain: Countries still differ: 16-year-olds vote in Scottish and Welsh elections, Australians must vote or pay a small fine, and some countries let prisoners vote while others do not.
explain: Children cannot vote, but decisions about schools, parks and the planet affect them for longest, so they can still learn, ask questions, write to leaders and talk to the grown-ups who do vote.
fact: In most countries the voting age is 18; it is a line drawn at an age, not a test of how much you know.
fact: In Scotland and Wales, 16- and 17-year-olds can vote in their own parliament and council elections; Austria also lets 16-year-olds vote.
fact: In 2025 it was reported that the UK government planned to lower the voting age to 16 for all UK elections.
fact: Suffrage means the right to vote; New Zealand gave women the vote in 1893, the USA in 1920, and the UK partly in 1918 and on equal terms with men in 1928.
fact: Usually only citizens can vote for a country's government, but there are exceptions: in the UK, Irish and Commonwealth citizens who live there can vote in general elections.
fact: In many countries you must register to vote, which puts your name on the official voters' list (the electoral register or electoral roll); this helps stop people voting twice.
fact: In Australia voting is compulsory for adult citizens: if you do not vote and have no good reason, you may get a small fine, though you can still hand in a blank paper.
fact: In the UK, the USA and New Zealand, voting is a free choice, not a duty.
fact: Turnout means how many people actually voted; in many elections only about half to two-thirds of the people who could vote do so.
fact: Being famous or rich does not give you a vote: the rule is one person, one vote, decided by things like age and citizenship.
fact: In the UK most people in prison cannot vote; in Ireland and Canada they can; people argue about which is right.
fact: Since 2023, voters in Great Britain must show photo ID at general elections; supporters say it makes cheating harder, critics say this kind of cheating was already very rare and some honest people have no ID.
fact: A youth parliament is a group of young people elected to speak for other young people.
exercise: Kai knows more about planets than Uncle Steve. Why can't Kai vote? | The rule says you must be 18, whatever you know | Kai would have to pass a hard science test first | Only uncles and aunts are allowed to vote in elections | Children can vote, but only for their own teachers
exercise: Fatima's 16-year-old cousin voted in Scotland. How could she? | Scotland lets 16- and 17-year-olds vote in its own elections | Scotland lets anyone vote there as long as they are tall enough | She broke the rules and nobody noticed | Every country in the world lets 16-year-olds vote
exercise: Why was Ruby's great-great-gran not allowed to vote? | Back then many countries did not let women vote | She did not have a phone or a computer to vote with | She lived much too far away from the polling station | Grans have never been allowed to vote anywhere at all
exercise: Which country gave women the vote first of these? | New Zealand, in 1893 | The USA, in 1920 | The UK, in 1928 | None of them before 1950
exercise: Priya's neighbour from India lives in the UK. Which is true about voting there? | Voting usually depends on citizenship, though Commonwealth citizens living in the UK can vote | Anyone who has lived in the UK for one week can vote | Nobody born outside the UK can ever vote anywhere | Only people with British grandparents can vote
exercise: Noah's brother turns 18. What must he usually do before voting? | Register, so his name is on the list of voters | Pass a driving test at the local school | Get a letter from the prime minister | Nothing, he just turns up on the day and shouts his vote
exercise: Why do many countries keep a list of voters? | To help stop people voting twice or in the wrong place | To send every voter a birthday card from the government | To choose which voters win a prize | To make sure only rich people vote
exercise: Aroha's Australian cousin says his parents could be fined for not voting. Why? | Voting is compulsory for adult citizens in Australia | Australians must pay to vote each time | His parents forgot to buy a ballot ticket | Fines are given to anyone who votes too early in the morning
exercise: Only about half of people voted, says Jake's dad. What is that number called? | Turnout | Landslide | Franchise | Recount
exercise: Mia's teacher says none of the class could vote in a real election. Why does it still matter to them? | Decisions about schools, parks and the planet affect them longest | Children will be given the results to count | Elections are only ever about things for grown-ups, like taxes and jobs | It does not matter to children at all
exercise: A famous footballer from abroad can't vote where he plays. What does this show? | Fame does not buy a vote; the same rules apply to everyone | Footballers are banned from every election | Only goalkeepers are allowed to vote | He must score fifty goals in the league before he can vote
exercise: Which is true about prisoners voting? | It differs: most UK prisoners cannot vote, but in Ireland and Canada they can | Every country lets every prisoner vote | No country has ever let a prisoner vote | Prisoners vote twice to make up for lost time
exercise: Why did Great Britain bring in photo ID for voting? | To make sure people are who they say they are | To give everyone a free photo of themselves to keep | To stop small children from going into polling stations | To make voting quicker for people who are in a hurry
exercise: What do critics of voter ID say? | Honest people without ID may be stopped, and this cheating was already rare | Photo ID makes cheating much easier to do | Nobody in the country owns a photograph | ID cards cost more than an election
exercise: What can children do in politics before they can vote? | Learn, ask questions, write to leaders and talk to adults who vote | Nothing at all until the day they turn 18 | Vote secretly on polling day by using their parents' names and cards | Choose the prime minister in a school assembly
test: Your mate says, "If ur smart enough u can vote at 10, no cap." What's the truth? | The voting age is a fixed rule, not a cleverness test | He's right, there's a quiz you take at the polling station | He's right, but only in the summer holidays when schools close | Anyone can vote if a teacher signs a note to say they're clever
test: A Welsh kid says, "I'm 16 and I'm proper tamping I can't vote in the general election!" ("Tamping" means furious.) What is true? | In Wales she can vote at 16 in Welsh elections, but UK general elections have used 18 | She can vote in every election in the world | She cannot vote in anything until she is 21 | Nobody in Wales is allowed to vote
test: A cartoon shows women voting since forever. What should be put right? | Many countries only let women vote from about 100 years ago, after long campaigns | Women never voted until last year | Only women were allowed to vote in the old days | Cartoons are always right about history
test: In Sydney, Kai's dad says, "Yeah nah, I'm not voting this time." ("Yeah nah" means no.) What could happen? | He could get a small fine, because voting is compulsory in Australia | Nothing, voting in Australia is just for fun and nobody checks at all | He would go straight to prison for life | Someone else would vote for him by law
test: What would happen if a country let only rich people vote? | Leaders would mostly listen to the rich and others would have no say | Everyone would be equally represented anyway | Poor people would be given two votes each instead, to make it fair | Elections would stop being needed at all
test: Grandad says, "In my day we showed nothing. Now photo ID? Don't have a cow, it's to stop cheating." ("Don't have a cow" means calm down.) Which is fair? | ID aims to stop cheating; others say it was rare and may stop honest voters | ID was invented to make grandads cross | ID means cheating is now impossible forever | ID is only needed for postal votes
test: Jake asks, "Why wouldn't u vote if u could?" Which is a real reason people give? | They feel unsure, busy, or unhappy with every choice | Voting costs one hundred pounds each time, paid at the door | People are not allowed to vote twice in a row, so they skip | Polling stations only open at midnight when most are asleep
test: Your teacher asks, "Should prisoners vote?" A classmate says, "Obvs not, no country lets them." What's wrong? | Some countries, like Ireland and Canada, do let them | Nothing, no country has ever let them vote | Every country forces prisoners to vote | Prisoners can only vote for the prison guards who look after them
test: Your cousin, being a bit sus (suspicious), says a pop star got "VIP votes" in an election. Is that how it works? | No, it's one person, one vote, famous or not | Yes, famous people get ten votes each, by law | Yes, but only famous singers, not famous actors | No, famous people are not allowed to vote at all
test: Your sister sighs, "Formally speaking, I must register before I vote? Ugh, adulting." ("Adulting" means doing boring grown-up jobs.) Why must she? | To get her name on the voters' list, which stops double voting | So the government can send her a free sticker | Because registering already counts as her vote for the whole election | So she can choose which school to vote in
test: A girl in a youth parliament says, "We can't vote, so we're wasting our time." What's the best reply? | You can still speak for young people and talk to the adults who decide | She is right, nobody listens to anyone under 18 | Youth parliaments choose the prime minister | Young people can vote in real elections as long as they are members of it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the minimum voting age in the majority of jurisdictions? | A threshold fixed by law at a given age, irrespective of knowledge or ability | An assessment of the intellectual capacity of each prospective elector in turn | A privilege conferred upon persons of fame or wealth | A provision that varies according to the height of the individual
exam: Pursuant to the law of the Commonwealth of Australia, ascertain the consequence of an eligible adult citizen's failure to vote without valid reason. | The imposition of a modest financial penalty | Mandatory imprisonment for a term of several years | The forfeiture of citizenship forthwith | No consequence of any description
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the extension of the franchise to women. | New Zealand in 1893; the United States in 1920; the United Kingdom partially in 1918 and equally in 1928 | The United Kingdom in 1893; New Zealand in 1928; the United States in 1918 | All three jurisdictions simultaneously in the year 1900 | None of the said jurisdictions prior to the year 1990
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the electoral register. | The prevention of multiple or misplaced voting by maintaining a list of eligible electors | The recording of the political preferences of every elector | The selection of candidates on behalf of political parties | The levying of taxation upon persons who vote
